<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christmas carol</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as noel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> carol is a song or hymn on the theme of Christmas, traditionally sung at Christmas or in the surrounding season. The first known Christmas hymns may be traced to 4th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and by the 13th century popular Christmas songs in vernacular languages had developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-of-assisi">Francis of Assisi</a>. The earliest English carols appear in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of 1426 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a> chaplain John Awdlay, who recorded twenty-five. Carols grew in popularity after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>, when reformers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> promoted congregational singing, and printed collections of the 19th century widened their appeal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christmas-carol.jpg" alt="Christmas carol" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prathyush Thomas · GFDL 1.2 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christmas_carol_group_BNC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term noel has sometimes been used, especially for carols of French origin. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> hymns, such as Veni redemptor gentium by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a>, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, were austere statements of the doctrine of the Incarnation against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>, and Corde natus ex Parentis by the Spanish poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prudentius">Prudentius</a> is still sung in some churches. In the 9th and 10th centuries the Christmas sequence emerged in monasteries of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and in the 12th century the Parisian monk Adam of Saint Victor began to add melodic elements from popular songs.</p><p>The songs now called carols were originally communal festival songs of seasonal celebrations such as harvesttide, and only later were adopted for church use. It is not clear whether the word carol comes from the French carole or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> carula, meaning a circular dance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Many carols were printed in Piae Cantiones, a collection of late-medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> songs first published in 1582, which includes early Latin forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-i-duke-of-bohemia">Good King Wenceslas</a>. Almost all the well-known carols were not sung in church until the second half of the 19th century, when Hymns Ancient and Modern included several. How All the Welkin Rings became Hark! the Herald Angels Sing, set to a tune from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-mendelssohn">Felix Mendelssohn</a>'s cantata Festgesang of 1840, adapted by William H. Cummings.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silent-night">Silent Night</a> was first performed in the Nikolauskirche in Oberndorf on 24 December 1818, with words by Joseph Mohr from 1816 and a melody and guitar accompaniment by Franz Xaver Gruber. Some carols can be traced to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/personent-hodie">Personent hodie</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-i-duke-of-bohemia">Good King Wenceslas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-holly-and-the-ivy">The Holly and the Ivy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>During the Puritan ban on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, carols were sung privately despite official restrictions. In the 19th century William Sandys's collection of 1833 included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-rest-ye-merry-gentlemen">God Rest Ye Merry, Gentlemen</a>, The First Noel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/i-saw-three-ships">I Saw Three Ships</a>, and Christmas Carols, New and Old by Henry Ramsden Bramley and John Stainer, published in 1871, contributed to a revival in Victorian Britain. The Oxford Book of Carols, first published in 1928 and edited by Martin Shaw, Ralph Vaughan Williams and Percy Dearmer, was widely used by choirs and congregations.</p><p>Some songs called carols are not religious: A Bone, God Wot! appears to be a wassailing song, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greensleeves">Greensleeves</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> words in 1865 as What Child is This?. A sociological study of caroling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> in the early 21st century found that the sources of songs are often misunderstood and that caroling also reinforces diverse national customs and local family traditions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christmas carol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The earliest English <b>carols</b> appear in a manuscript of 1426 by the Shropshire chaplain John Awdlay, who recorded twenty-five.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>The first known Christmas hymns may be traced to 4th-century Rome, and by the 13th century popular Christmas songs in vernacular languages had developed in France, Germany and particularly Italy; the earliest English examples of the <b>Christmas carol</b> appear in a manuscript of 1426.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>Although the plays were performed in summer, the lullaby is now sung as a <b>Christmas carol</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/coventry-carol">Coventry Carol</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Deck the Halls&quot; is a <b>Christmas carol</b> with a Welsh tune and English words.</blockquote><a href="/songs/deck-the-halls">Deck the Halls</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Es ist ein Ros entsprungen&quot; (&quot;A rose has sprung up&quot;) is a <b>Christmas carol</b> and Marian hymn of German origin, best known in English as &quot;Lo, How a Rose E'er Blooming&quot; and also called &quot;A Spotless Rose&quot; and &quot;Behold a Rose of Judah&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/es-ist-ein-ros-entsprungen">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Gaudete&quot; (&quot;Rejoice&quot;) is a sacred <b>Christmas carol</b> in Latin, thought to have been composed in the 16th century, though there is a known entry from around 1420 in the Hussite Jistebnice hymnal (Jistebnický kancionál).</blockquote><a href="/songs/gaudete">Gaudete</a></li></ul></section>
